File: emacsvox.info,  Node: emacsvox-eww,  Next: emacsvox-extras,  Prev: emacsvox-evil,  Up: Emacsvox Commands And Options

14.117 emacsvox-eww
===================

EWW == Emacs Web Browser EWW is a light-weight Web browser built into
Emacs starting with Emacs-24.4 .  This module speech-enables EWW.

   It implements additional interactive commands for navigating the DOM.
It also provides a set of filters for interactively filtering the DOM by
various attributes such as id, class and role.  Finally, this module
updates EWW's built-in key-bindings with Emacsvox conveniences -- for a
complete list of key-bindings, invoke command ‘describe-bindings’ in an
‘EWW’ buffer by pressing ‘C-h b’.

14.117.1 Structured Navigation
------------------------------

These commands move through section headers as defined in HTML.
‘1’
     ‘emacsvox-eww-next-h1’ Move to next ‘H1’ heading.
‘2’
     ‘emacsvox-eww-next-h2’ Move to next ‘H2’ heading.
‘3’
     ‘emacsvox-eww-next-h3’ Move to next ‘H3’ heading.
‘4’
     ‘emacsvox-eww-next-h4’ Move to next ‘H4’ heading.
‘.’
     ‘emacsvox-eww-next-h’ Move to next heading.  (‘H1’...‘H4’).
‘M-1’
     ‘emacsvox-eww-previous-h1’ Move to previous ‘H1’ heading.
‘M-2’
     ‘emacsvox-eww-previous-h2’ Move to previous ‘H2’ heading.
‘M-3’
     ‘emacsvox-eww-previous-h3’ Move to previous ‘H3’ heading.
‘M-4’
     ‘emacsvox-eww-previous-h4’ Move to previous ‘H4’ heading.
‘,’
     ‘emacsvox-eww-previous-h’ Move to previous heading (‘H1’...‘H4’).

   This next set of DOM commands enable navigating by HTML elements.
‘M-SPC’
     ‘emacsvox-eww-speak-this-element’ Speak contents of current
     element.
‘J’
     ‘emacsvox-eww-next-element-like-this’ Jump to next element that is
     the same as the one under point.  If there are multiple HTML
     elements under point, prompts for element-name using completion.
‘K’
     ‘emacsvox-eww-previous-element-like-this’ Jump to previous element
     that is the same as the one under point.  If there are multiple
     HTML elements under point, prompts for element-name using
     completion.
‘N’
     ‘emacsvox-eww-next-element-from-history’ Jump to next element based
     on previous J/K command history.
‘P’
     ‘emacsvox-eww-previous-element-from-history’ Jump to previous
     element based on previous J/K history.
‘O’
     ‘emacsvox-eww-previous-li’ Jump to previous list item.
‘o’
     ‘emacsvox-eww-next-li’ Jump to next list item.
‘T’
     ‘emacsvox-eww-previous-table’ Jump to previous table in page.
‘t’
     ‘emacsvox-eww-next-table’ Jump to next table.
‘[’
     ‘emacsvox-eww-previous-p’ Jump to previous paragraph.
‘]’
     ‘emacsvox-eww-next-p’ Jump to next paragraph.
‘b’
     ‘shr-previous-link’ Jump to previous link.
‘f’
     ‘shr-next-link’ Jump to next link.
‘n’
     ‘emacsvox-eww-next-element’ Jump to next element.
‘p’
     ‘emacsvox-eww-previous-element’ Jump to previous element.
‘s’
     ‘eww-readable’ Use EWW's built-in readable tool.
‘:’
     ‘emacsvox-eww-tags-at-point’ Display currently active HTML tags at
     point.

14.117.2 Filtering Content Using The DOM
----------------------------------------

These commands use EWW's HTML DOM to display different filtered views of
the Web page.  With an interactive prefix argument, these commands
prompt for a list of filters.  Command ‘emacsvox-eww-restore’ bound to
‘DEL’ can be used to restore the previous view.

‘A’
     ‘eww-view-dom-having-attribute’ Display DOM nodes having specified
     attribute.  Valid attributes are available via completion.
‘C’
     ‘eww-view-dom-having-class’ Display DOM nodes having specified
     class.  Valid classes are available via completion.
‘E’
     ‘eww-view-dom-having-elements’ Display specified elements from the
     Dom.  Valid element names are available via completion.
‘I’
     ‘eww-view-dom-having-id’ Display DOM nodes having specified ID.
     Valid id values are available via completion.

‘M’
     ‘eww-view-dom-element-having-text’ lines containing pattern.
     Useful to filter down RSS feeds.
‘R’
     ‘eww-view-dom-having-role’ Display DOM nodes having specified role.
     Valid roles are available via completion.
‘M-a’
     ‘eww-view-dom-not-having-attribute’ Filter out DOM nodes having
     specified attribute.  Valid attribute values are available via
     completion.
‘M-c’
     ‘eww-view-dom-not-having-class’ Filter out DOM nodes having
     specified class.  Valid class values are available via completion.
‘M-e’
     ‘eww-view-dom-not-having-elements’ Filter out specified element DOM
     nodes.  Valid element names are available via completion.
‘M-i’
     ‘eww-view-dom-not-having-id’ Dfilter out Display DOM nodes having
     specified ID. Valid id values are available via completion.
‘M-r’
     ‘eww-view-dom-not-having-role’ Filter out DOM nodes having
     specified role.  Valid role values are available via completion.

14.117.3 Diving Into (Focusing) On Specific Content
---------------------------------------------------

Contrast this with filtering described in the previous section.  There,
we discussed commands that *filter* the DOM to render specific types of
elements.  For HTML as spoken on the Web, there is a separate use-case
that is helpful as a dual to filtering, namely, displaying a specific
portion of a page, typically the contents of a ‘div’ element.  These
elements often appear many times on a page, and can be deeply nested,
making it difficult to focus on the relevant content on the page, e.g.
news sites.  Commands ‘emacsvox-eww-dive-into-div’ help in such cases,
‘C-d’ renders the ‘div’ containing point in a separate buffer As with
the filtering commands, ‘l’ returns to the buffer where these commands
were executed.  Long-term users of Emacsvox who still remember Emacs-W3
will recognize this as the *focus* command implemented by Emacsvox for
W3.

14.117.4 Updated Commands For Following Links
---------------------------------------------

These key-bindings are available when point is on a link.  They enable
context-specific actions for following links, e.g., to play media
streams, or to open various feed-types such as ‘ATOM’, ‘RSS’, or ‘OPML’.

‘k’
     ‘shr-copy-url’ Copy URL under point to the kill-ring.
‘;’
     ‘emacsvox-eww-play-media-at-point’ Play media URL under point using
     ‘emacs-m-player’.  Handles URL fragment as time-stamp where we
     resume; use ‘J’ in M-Player to jump to that offset.
‘u’
     ‘emacsvox-eww-url-to-register’ Accumulate url under point to
     register‘u’.  Sample use-cases include building up a playlist of
     links in the right sort order after a YT search.
‘x’
     ‘emacsvox-feeds-select-feed’ Display link under point as an ‘ATOM’,
     ‘OPML’ or ‘RSS’ feed.
‘y’
     ‘empv-play’ Play link -under point as a Youtube stream.

14.117.5 Table Browsing
-----------------------

Summary Of Keyboard Commands:
   • ‘M-<left>’ emacsvox-eww-table-previous-cell -- Speak previous cell.
   • ‘M-<right>’ emacsvox-eww-table-next-cell -- Speak previous cell.
   • ‘M-<up>’ emacsvox-eww-table-previous-row -- Speak cell above.
   • ‘M-<down>’ emacsvox-eww-table-next-row -- Speak cell below.
   • ‘M-.’ emacsvox-eww-table-speak-cell -- Speak current cell.
   • ‘M-,’ emacsvox-eww-table-speak-dimensions -- Speak number of rows
     and columns.
   • ‘C-t’ emacsvox-eww-table-data -- Browse this table in Emacsvox's
     Table UI -- *Note emacsvox-table-ui::.

   Emacsvox EWW supports table navigation via keys ‘M-.’, ‘M-LEFT’ and
‘M-RIGHT’, to speak the current, previous and next table cell
respectively.  The latter commands also move to the cell being spoken.
You can get a sense of the table's size via ‘M-,’ which speaks the
number of rows and cells in the table.  This works for plain tables, not
nested tables; for nested tables, first have then *unnested* using one
of the XSLT transforms like ‘sort-tables’.

